### Slide 1 (0:00 to 0:15)

I am Philip, and this is Zelos: every organ-on-a-chip image, certified. It finds the imaging fault, names the lab fix, cites open literature, and blocks every claim the evidence cannot support.

### Slide 3 (0:15 to 0:45)

Each image gets a certificate: a verdict, a route, the cause, the lab action, the cited passage, and a ledger where every sentence is linked to a tool output or a paper. Here a real image with an injected over-exposure fails and is routed to re-imaging; an untouched image passes. Where our corpus does not support an action, the certificate says so.

### Slide 4 (0:45 to 1:05)

The rule is: the LLM proposes, tools compute, the gate disposes. {{n_metrics}} physics metrics decide the verdict and the cause with no model involved. The language model only proposes sentences, and a deterministic gate checks every number and every citation.

### Slide 5 (1:05 to 1:30)

The gate caught {{gate.caught}} injected fabrications and blocked {{gate.false_block}} correct claims. Unsupported claims in model answers fall from {{e5.unsup_un}} to {{e5.unsup_g}}, and unsafe adversarial outputs from {{e5.unsafe_un}} to {{e5.unsafe_g}}. A bigger model did not help.

### Slide 6 (1:30 to 1:55)

On injected faults with a known answer, Zelos flags {{e1.batch.det3}} of the severity {{sev.mid}} and {{sev.top}} faults at {{e1.batch.clean}} clean false flags, and names the cause {{e1.batch.sig}} of the time against {{e1.chance}} by chance. It mostly misses {{miss_list}}, and I say so.

### Slide 8 (1:55 to 2:20)

The main finding: absolute thresholds do not transfer between imaging sessions. Left out of calibration, whole sessions get {{cal.pooled.loso}} and {{cal.between.loso}} false flags against a {{target_ff}} target; scoring each image against its own session gets {{cal.batch.loso}}.

### Slide 9 (2:20 to 2:35)

The expert good-or-bad label tracks culture state, not image faults: our artifact score is at chance, {{e2.qc_batch.auroc}}. We do not claim state-of-the-art expert-label accuracy.

### Slide 13 (2:35 to 2:50)

Corrections: the submission, v1.0, used {{v1.n_images}} of {{n_images}} images and a reference that flagged {{v1.clean_flag}} of clean images. v1.1 is a post-deadline build that fixes both, and its detection rate is lower because the operating point is now honest.

### Slide 15 (2:50 to 3:00)

Every sentence Zelos writes traces to a tool or a paper. Research tool, not a medical device. Thank you.

